"""Manuscript content. Inline markup: *italic*, **bold**, _{sub}, ^{sup}. Citations: {key} or {k1,k2}."""
import json, re

TITLE = "Explainable Feature-Token Transformers for Network Intrusion Detection: Rigorous Evaluation, False-Alarm Control and Explanation Reliability"
AUTHOR = "Xinghui Wu"
AFFIL = "School of Computer Science, University of Birmingham, Birmingham B15 2TT, UK; [e-mail address]"

ABSTRACT = ("Transformer-based network intrusion detection systems (NIDSs) are often reported with near-perfect accuracy, yet such results are frequently obtained under single-run protocols, with data leakage, decision thresholds that ignore false-alarm cost, and explanations whose reliability is never tested. This study revisits a feature-token Transformer, in which every flow feature becomes a token processed by self-attention, under a protocol designed to remove these weaknesses. "
 "On UNSW-NB15 and TON_IoT, exact duplicates (38.6% and 55.6% of the source files) were removed before splitting; all models were tuned and thresholded on validation data only; five baselines (random forest, XGBoost, MLP, 1D-CNN and FT-Transformer) and five ablations were run with five seeds and paired significance tests. "
 "Accuracy was statistically indistinguishable from or slightly below gradient-boosted trees, whereas a decision threshold set at a 1% validation false-alarm budget reduced the UNSW-NB15 test false-positive rate from 26.6% to 4.4% and raised F1 from 89.5% to 93.7%, a gain that held for every model and on de-duplicated test data. "
 "Expected-gradients SHAP explanations were faithful (masking the top five features reduced ROC-AUC from 0.984 to 0.738, versus 0.980 for random features) but only moderately stable across retrained models (Spearman 0.76). Error analysis showed that false alarms stem from a train–test shift for which TTL features act as proxies; removing those features did not remove the shift. "
 "The results indicate that explainable NIDS research should report operating-point false-alarm rates and explanation reliability alongside accuracy.")
KEYWORDS = "network intrusion detection; Transformer; explainable artificial intelligence; SHAP; false alarms; dataset shift; UNSW-NB15; TON_IoT; Internet of Things security"

B = []  # blocks
def h1(t): B.append({"t": "h1", "x": t})
def h2(t): B.append({"t": "h2", "x": t})
def p(t): B.append({"t": "p", "x": t})
def eq(t, n): B.append({"t": "eq", "x": t, "n": n})
def bullet(t): B.append({"t": "li", "x": t})
def fig(f, cap): B.append({"t": "fig", "f": f, "cap": cap})
def table(cap, head, rows, widths, note=None, small=True): B.append({"t": "tab", "cap": cap, "head": head, "rows": rows, "w": widths, "note": note, "small": small})

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 1
h1("1. Introduction")
p("Network intrusion detection systems (NIDSs) monitor traffic to identify activity that threatens the confidentiality, integrity or availability of networked systems {khraisat}. Signature-based detectors are precise for known attacks but cannot recognise novel ones, which has motivated two decades of research on machine-learning (ML) and deep-learning (DL) detectors that learn the boundary between benign and malicious flows from labelled data {buczak,yin}. The growth of Internet of Things (IoT) and edge deployments, where heterogeneous devices generate large volumes of telemetry and network flows, has made such data-driven monitoring an increasingly important component of sensor and IoT security {moustafa_ton,booij}.")
p("Two observations temper the optimism around learned NIDSs. First, operational value is determined less by accuracy than by the false-alarm rate: because benign traffic vastly outnumbers attacks, even a detector with a low false-positive rate (FPR) can bury analysts in alerts {axelsson,sommer}. Second, many reported results do not survive careful scrutiny. Recurrent pitfalls include data snooping, inappropriate baselines, single-run evaluation and spurious correlations that let a model exploit artefacts of the data-collection process rather than attack behaviour {arp,apruzzese}. Benchmark datasets themselves contain duplicated records, labelling problems and distributional differences between their predefined partitions {engelen,zoghi_vis,zoghi_cpe}. Explainable artificial intelligence (XAI) is frequently proposed as a remedy, yet explanation methods are themselves fallible: different methods disagree, explanations can be unstable, and their faithfulness to the model must be measured rather than assumed {warnecke,krishna,alvarez,maseno}.")
p("Transformers {vaswani} are an attractive architecture for flow-level NIDSs because self-attention can model interactions between heterogeneous flow attributes. In the feature-token formulation popularised for tabular data by the FT-Transformer {gorishniy}, each feature is embedded as a separate token so that attention operates across features of a single record. A growing number of Transformer-based NIDSs report very high detection rates on UNSW-NB15, TON_IoT, CIC-IDS2017 and related benchmarks {wu_rtids,ullah,umer,zhang2025}. At the same time, a large body of tabular-learning research shows that gradient-boosted decision trees remain difficult to beat on typical tabular data {grinsztajn,shwartz}. Whether attention-based models improve flow-level detection, and whether their explanations can be trusted, therefore requires evaluation under protocols that are strict enough to expose these effects.")
p("This paper extends the author's MSc project on a SHAP-explained feature-token Transformer for UNSW-NB15. Rather than proposing yet another architecture, it asks three questions that a practitioner would ask before deploying such a model. (i) Under a leakage-free, multi-seed protocol, how does a feature-token Transformer compare with strong tree-based and neural baselines, and which of its components matter? (ii) Can false alarms be controlled at deployment time using only information available before testing? (iii) Are the SHAP explanations of the model faithful and stable enough to support analyst decisions, and what do they reveal about the model's errors? The main contributions are as follows:")
bullet("**A rigorous evaluation protocol** for flow-based NIDSs, applied to UNSW-NB15 and TON_IoT: exact-duplicate removal before splitting, validation-only model selection and threshold setting, five random seeds, McNemar and paired-bootstrap significance tests, and a test-set sensitivity analysis. The protocol exposes that 38.6% of the UNSW-NB15 training file and 55.6% of the TON_IoT network file are exact duplicates.")
bullet("**A controlled comparison and ablation study** of feature-token Transformers against random forest (RF), XGBoost, multilayer perceptron (MLP), one-dimensional convolutional network (1D-CNN) and FT-Transformer baselines. The feature-token model is competitive but not superior to gradient-boosted trees; the feature-identity embedding is its critical component on both datasets, while the choice of numerical and categorical embedding matters little.")
bullet("**A validation-budgeted decision rule** that fixes the operating threshold at a target validation FPR. On UNSW-NB15 it reduces the test FPR of every model by 18–23 percentage points (from 26.6% to 4.4% for the feature-token model) and raises F1 by 3.6–4.2 points, whereas conventional F1- or Youden-optimal thresholds do not reduce false alarms.")
bullet("**An explanation-reliability audit** that quantifies SHAP faithfulness, stability across backgrounds and retrained models, agreement with permutation importance, and SHAP profiles of true and false positives. It shows that the model's false alarms are driven by time-to-live (TTL) features that act as proxies for a train–test shift, and that removing those features leaves both the false alarms and their population unchanged.")
p("The remainder of the paper is organised as follows. Section 2 reviews related work. Section 3 describes the datasets, protocol, models, decision rules and explanation analysis. Section 4 reports the results, Section 5 discusses their implications and limitations, and Section 6 concludes.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 2
h1("2. Related Work")
h2("2.1. Benchmark Datasets and Evaluation Pitfalls")
p("UNSW-NB15 was generated with the IXIA PerfectStorm tool to combine contemporary benign traffic with nine attack families, and it ships with predefined training (175,341 records) and testing (82,332 records) partitions {moustafa2015,moustafa2016}. TON_IoT extends this line of work to IoT and IIoT testbeds with telemetry, operating-system and network data, the latter described by Zeek-derived flow features {moustafa_ton,booij}. Newer IoT datasets such as Edge-IIoTset and CICIoT2023 follow similar designs {ferrag,neto}. Engelen et al. showed that labelling and feature-extraction errors in CIC-IDS2017 substantially alter reported performance {engelen}, and Zoghi and Serpen documented class overlap, imbalance and record duplication in UNSW-NB15 {zoghi_vis,zoghi_cpe}. Sarhan et al. demonstrated that feature sets that differ across datasets hamper the generalisation and comparability of NIDS models {sarhan}. More broadly, Arp et al. catalogued ten pitfalls in ML-based security research, including sampling bias, data snooping, spurious correlations, inappropriate baselines and inappropriate performance measures {arp}, and Apruzzese et al. argued that NIDS evaluations must reflect operational constraints such as false-alarm budgets {apruzzese}. These concerns echo the earlier warnings of Sommer and Paxson {sommer} and Axelsson's base-rate analysis {axelsson}. The present study adopts these recommendations as explicit protocol choices and quantifies their effect.")
h2("2.2. Transformers for Tabular and Flow-Based Intrusion Detection")
p("Self-attention {vaswani} has been adapted to tabular data either by contextualising categorical embeddings only, as in TabTransformer {huang}, or by tokenising every feature, as in FT-Transformer {gorishniy}. In intrusion detection, RTIDS combined positional embeddings with stacked encoder–decoder layers for CIC-IDS2017 and CIC-DDoS2019 {wu_rtids}; IDS-INT combined Transformer-based transfer learning with SMOTE, CNN and LSTM components and added XAI {ullah}; Umer et al. paired wrapper-based feature selection with a multi-head attention Transformer on UNSW-NB15 {umer}; and Zhang et al. fused a Transformer with a CNN-BiLSTM for IoT intrusion detection {zhang2025}. Most of these studies report a single run, tune on data that overlap with the reported test data, or compare against untuned baselines. Benchmarks of tabular learning find that tree ensembles such as random forests {breiman} and XGBoost {chen_xgb} remain at least as accurate as deep models on medium-sized tabular datasets {grinsztajn,shwartz}. A fair assessment of attention-based NIDSs therefore requires tuned tree-based baselines and repeated runs.")
h2("2.3. Explainable Intrusion Detection and Explanation Reliability")
p("SHAP attributes a prediction to input features through Shapley values {lundberg}, and its gradient-based variant uses expected gradients to explain differentiable models efficiently {erion}. XAI-based NIDSs commonly use SHAP or LIME to rank features globally or to explain individual alerts {neupane,sharma}. Explanations, however, can be unfaithful or unstable. Warnecke et al. proposed descriptive accuracy, sparsity, stability and efficiency criteria for explanations in security applications {warnecke}; Hooker et al. showed that feature-removal benchmarks are needed to verify that attributions identify genuinely important inputs {hooker}; Alvarez-Melis and Jaakkola reported that small input perturbations can change explanations {alvarez}; and Krishna et al. documented systematic disagreement between explanation methods {krishna}. Most recently, Maseno et al. audited the reliability of SHAP explanations for tree-based and linear NIDSs on UNSW-NB15 and TON_IoT using consistency, faithfulness and retraining-stability metrics {maseno}. The present work complements that audit by examining a deep feature-token Transformer, by measuring stability across independently trained models, and by linking explanations to the model's false alarms. This connects XAI to the literature on shortcut learning, in which models exploit spurious cues that do not generalise {geirhos,lapuschkin}, and on dataset shift {moreno}.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 3
h1("3. Materials and Methods")
p("Figure 1 summarises the study design. All code, configurations and per-run outputs are released (see the Data Availability Statement), and every experiment can be reproduced from a single entry-point script.")
fig("fig1_framework.png", "Overview of the evaluation framework. Stage I prepares leakage-free data; Stage II trains the feature-token Transformer and five baselines with five seeds; Stage III fixes the decision threshold on validation data only; Stage IV audits the SHAP explanations.")
h2("3.1. Datasets")
p("**UNSW-NB15.** The official training and testing files were used {moustafa2015}. Each record has 42 features after removing the record identifier, the attack-category column and the binary label: 39 numerical features and three categorical features (*proto*, *service* and *state*). The task is binary classification of normal (label 0) versus attack (label 1) flows. The official test file was kept unchanged so that results remain comparable with the literature; it contains 37,000 normal and 45,332 attack records (55.1% attacks).")
p("**TON_IoT.** The *Train_Test_Network* file of the TON_IoT network dataset was used {moustafa_ton}. It contains 211,043 records (50,000 normal and 161,043 attacks across nine attack types). Source and destination IP addresses and ports were removed because they identify testbed hosts rather than behaviour, as were five high-cardinality free-text fields (*dns_query*, *ssl_subject*, *ssl_issuer*, *http_uri* and *http_user_agent*). The remaining 33 features comprise 14 numerical and 19 categorical features. Because TON_IoT has no predefined test partition, the de-duplicated data were split 60/20/20 into training, validation and test sets, stratified by attack type.")
h2("3.2. Leakage-Free Data Protocol")
p("Exact duplicates (identical feature values, label and attack category) were removed from the UNSW-NB15 training file and from the TON_IoT file **before** any split. Without this step, copies of the same record can fall on both sides of the training–validation split, inflating validation scores and biasing model and threshold selection. Table 1 lists the resulting partitions. In UNSW-NB15, 67,601 records (38.6%) of the training file were duplicates, mostly attacks; removing them changed the attack share of the training data from 68.1% to 51.8%. In TON_IoT, 117,367 records (55.6%) were duplicates once host identifiers had been removed. The UNSW-NB15 training file was then split 80/20 into training and validation sets, stratified by label, with a fixed split seed of 42. The same split was used for every model and every training seed.")
table("Table 1. Data partitions after de-duplication. Percentages give the share of attack records.",
      ["Dataset", "Partition", "Records", "Normal", "Attack", "Attack (%)", "Use"],
      [["UNSW-NB15", "Training file (raw)", "175,341", "56,000", "119,341", "68.1", "Source"],
       ["", "Exact duplicates removed", "67,601", "—", "—", "—", "—"],
       ["", "Training", "86,192", "41,512", "44,680", "51.8", "Fitting"],
       ["", "Validation", "21,548", "10,378", "11,170", "51.8", "Selection, threshold"],
       ["", "Official test", "82,332", "37,000", "45,332", "55.1", "Final evaluation"],
       ["TON_IoT", "Network file (raw)", "211,043", "50,000", "161,043", "76.3", "Source"],
       ["", "Exact duplicates removed", "117,367", "—", "—", "—", "—"],
       ["", "Training", "56,205", "14,202", "42,003", "74.7", "Fitting"],
       ["", "Validation", "18,735", "4,734", "14,001", "74.7", "Selection, threshold"],
       ["", "Test", "18,736", "4,735", "14,001", "74.7", "Final evaluation"]],
      [1200, 1900, 1000, 900, 900, 900, 1600])
p("Preprocessing statistics were estimated on the training partition only and then applied unchanged to the validation and test partitions. Numerical features were transformed with a signed logarithm, sign(*x*)·log(1+|*x*|), to compress heavy-tailed byte, packet and duration counts, and then standardised. Categorical values were mapped to integer indices in alphabetical order, with index 0 reserved for categories not seen during training (five UNSW-NB15 test records have an unseen *state* value). Models that consume a single numerical vector (the 1D-CNN, the single-token Transformer and the ablations with ordinal categoricals) receive the index minus one, standardised, as in the original MSc implementation. Tree models receive the raw numerical values and the ordinal codes, because they are invariant to monotone transformations.")
p("The UNSW-NB15 test file also contains duplicates (26,387 records, 32.0%), and 8,541 test records have a feature vector that also occurs in the training file. Duplicates in a test set do not leak information into training, but they weight the evaluation towards easily recognised attacks. The main results are therefore reported on the official test set, and Section 4.4 repeats the evaluation on a de-duplicated test set (55,945 records) and on a de-duplicated test set without training overlap (53,915 records).")

h2("3.3. Feature-Token Transformer")
p("Figure 2 shows the feature-token Transformer evaluated in this study, denoted FTT-IDS. Let a flow record consist of *m* numerical features *x*_{1}, …, *x*_{m} and *k* categorical features *c*_{1}, …, *c*_{k}. Each numerical feature is mapped to a *d*-dimensional token by a feature-specific affine map, and each categorical feature by a feature-specific embedding table:")
eq("t_{i} = x_{i} w_{i} + b_{i},   i = 1, …, m;      t_{m+j} = E_{j}[c_{j}],   j = 1, …, k,", 1)
p("where *w*_{i}, *b*_{i} ∈ ℝ^{d} and *E*_{j} ∈ ℝ^{(|C_{j}|+1)×d}. Because *b*_{i} differs for every feature, it also acts as a learned feature-identity embedding. The token matrix *T* ∈ ℝ^{(m+k)×d} is processed by an *L*-layer Transformer encoder with multi-head self-attention {vaswani}:")
eq("Attention(Q, K, V) = softmax(QK^{⊤} / √d_{h}) V,   with Q = TW_{Q}, K = TW_{K}, V = TW_{V},", 2)
p("followed by a position-wise feed-forward network, residual connections and layer normalisation. The encoder outputs are mean-pooled over the *m*+*k* tokens and passed to a linear layer that produces two logits, *o*_{normal} and *o*_{attack}; the attack probability is *p* = softmax(*o*)_{attack}. FTT-IDS uses *d* = 64, *L* = 2, four attention heads, a feed-forward width of 2048, dropout 0.1 and post-normalisation. These settings are those of the original MSc model, so that the study isolates the effect of the protocol and the embedding design rather than of capacity tuning.")
fig("fig2_architecture.png", "Architecture of the feature-token Transformer (FTT-IDS). Every numerical feature is mapped by its own affine map and every categorical feature by its own embedding table, so each flow becomes a sequence of *m*+*k* tokens processed by self-attention.")
p("The original MSc model differs in two respects: it uses a single affine map Linear(1→*d*) shared by all features, plus an explicit learnable feature-identity embedding added to each token, and it feeds categorical features as standardised ordinal numbers. Four ablations separate these choices (Table 2): (A1) the MSc model, with shared embedding, identity embedding and ordinal categoricals; (A2) A1 without the identity embedding; (A3) per-feature embedding with ordinal categoricals; and (A4) shared embedding with categorical embedding tables. A further control, (A5) a single-token Transformer, projects the whole feature vector into one token, as in the first experiment of the MSc project. With a sequence of length one, self-attention reduces to an identity mapping, so A5 tests whether attention across feature tokens is useful at all.")

h2("3.4. Baselines and Training")
p("Five baselines cover the three families that a reviewer would expect. **RF** {breiman} and **XGBoost** {chen_xgb} represent tree ensembles. The **MLP** has three hidden layers (256, 256, 128) with batch normalisation, ReLU and dropout, and it receives categorical features through 8-dimensional embeddings. The **1D-CNN** applies two convolutional layers (32 and 64 channels, kernel size 3) with batch normalisation, max pooling and a 128-unit dense layer to the standardised feature vector. **FT-Transformer** {gorishniy} follows the authors' default configuration (*d* = 192, three pre-norm layers, eight heads, ReGLU feed-forward blocks, attention dropout 0.2, CLS-token readout, AdamW). TabTransformer {huang} was not included because it contextualises only categorical features, which make up 3 of the 42 UNSW-NB15 features; a recurrent baseline was not included because flow features have no natural order.")
p("All neural models were trained with cross-entropy loss, batch size 256, at most 60 epochs and early stopping with patience 10 on validation loss; the checkpoint with the lowest validation loss was retained. Adam {kingma} was used for all neural models except FT-Transformer, which used AdamW {loshchilov} with weight decay 10^{−5}. Each model received a small hyperparameter grid of comparable size, evaluated on the validation set with training seed 42 and selected by validation ROC-AUC (Table 2). The ablations inherited the learning rate selected for the MSc model, so that only the architectural factor changes. Each final configuration was then trained with five seeds (42–46), which change parameter initialisation and mini-batch order but not the data split. Experiments ran in PyTorch {paszke} 2.11 with CUDA 12.8 on an NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5060 Laptop GPU (8 GB); scikit-learn {pedregosa} 1.9, XGBoost 3.4 and SHAP 0.52 were used for the tree models and explanations.")
table("Table 2. Models, tuning grids (validation set, seed 42) and selected values for UNSW-NB15 / TON_IoT.",
      ["Model", "Description", "Grid", "Selected"],
      [["FTT-IDS (ours)", "Per-feature numerical + categorical embeddings, d = 64, 2 layers, 4 heads", "lr ∈ {10^{−3}, 5·10^{−4}, 2·10^{−4}}", "5·10^{−4} / 10^{−3}"],
       ["A1: MSc model", "Shared Linear(1→d) + identity embedding, ordinal categoricals", "same as above", "10^{−3} / 10^{−3}"],
       ["A2", "A1 without identity embedding", "lr from A1", "—"],
       ["A3", "Per-feature embedding, ordinal categoricals", "lr from A1", "—"],
       ["A4", "Shared embedding + categorical embeddings", "lr from A1", "—"],
       ["A5: single token", "All features → one token (attention is an identity map)", "same as FTT-IDS", "5·10^{−4} / 2·10^{−4}"],
       ["RF", "300 trees", "min_samples_leaf ∈ {1, 5}; max_features ∈ {√p, 0.5}", "(5, 0.5) / (1, √p)"],
       ["XGBoost", "hist, lr 0.05, ≤3000 trees, early stopping 100", "max_depth ∈ {6, 8, 10}", "8 / 10"],
       ["MLP", "256-256-128, BN, ReLU", "lr ∈ {10^{−3}, 5·10^{−4}}; dropout ∈ {0.1, 0.3}", "(10^{−3}, 0.1) / (5·10^{−4}, 0.3)"],
       ["1D-CNN", "Conv(32)-Conv(64)-MaxPool-FC(128)", "lr ∈ {10^{−3}, 5·10^{−4}, 2·10^{−4}}", "10^{−3} / 10^{−3}"],
       ["FT-Transformer", "Default configuration of {gorishniy}", "lr ∈ {3·10^{−4}, 10^{−4}}", "10^{−4} / 10^{−4}"]],
      [1500, 3300, 2600, 1800])

h2("3.5. Decision Rules and Evaluation")
p("Every model outputs an attack probability *p*(*x*), and an alert is raised when *p*(*x*) ≥ τ. Four rules for choosing τ were compared, all of which use only validation data: the conventional τ = 0.5; τ_{F1}, which maximises validation F1; τ_{J}, which maximises Youden's index (TPR − FPR) on the validation ROC curve; and a false-alarm budget rule,")
eq("τ_{FPR}(α) = min { τ : FPR_{val}(τ) ≤ α },", 3)
p("which selects the most sensitive threshold whose validation false-positive rate does not exceed a budget α. The budget was set to α = 1% for all models and datasets and was not tuned. The rule formalises how a security operations team would configure a detector: the tolerable alert volume is fixed first, and detection is then maximised within that budget {axelsson,apruzzese}.")
p("Test performance is reported as accuracy, precision, recall (detection rate), F1, FPR, Matthews correlation coefficient (MCC), ROC-AUC {fawcett} and average precision (AP) {saito}, as mean ± standard deviation over the five seeds. Detection rates per attack category are reported for UNSW-NB15. Paired differences between FTT-IDS and each comparator were tested in two ways. McNemar's test {mcnemar,dietterich} was applied to the predictions of each seed pair; the median *p*-value and the number of seeds with *p* < 0.05 are reported. In addition, a paired bootstrap over test records {efron} with 500–1000 replicates was used: each replicate averages the metric over the five seeds of both models and yields a 95% confidence interval for the difference in F1 and ROC-AUC. A Wilcoxon test over seeds was not used, because with five seeds its smallest attainable two-sided *p*-value is 0.0625.")

h2("3.6. Explanation Analysis")
p("Explanations target the attack margin *f*(*x*) = *o*_{attack}(*x*) − *o*_{normal}(*x*), so that positive SHAP values push a flow towards an alert. SHAP values were computed with GradientExplainer, an implementation of expected gradients {lundberg,erion}, using 500 training records as background and 2000 test records as explanation set (both stratified by label, identical for all seeds) and 500 samples per explanation. For categorical features, attributions were computed in the embedding space and summed over embedding dimensions, so that each original feature receives one value. The additivity error |E[*f*] + Σ_{i} φ_{i} − *f*(*x*)|, which is non-zero for this sampling-based estimator, is reported as a diagnostic. The global importance of feature *i* is the mean absolute SHAP value over the explanation set, averaged over the five seeds.")
p("Four reliability properties were assessed. (i) **Stability across backgrounds**: the global ranking of one model was recomputed with two further background samples and compared using Spearman's ρ, Kendall's τ and the Jaccard overlap of the top-10 features. (ii) **Stability across retrained models**: the same statistics were computed for all ten pairs of the five seeds. (iii) **Faithfulness**: following feature-removal benchmarks {hooker,warnecke}, the *k* globally most important features were replaced by reference values (training mean for numerical features, training mode for categorical features) on the full test set without retraining, for *k* ∈ {1, 2, 3, 5, 10, 15, 20}; the drop in ROC-AUC was compared with masking *k* random features (five draws). A local variant masked each explained alert's own top-*k* features. (iv) **Agreement with a model-agnostic method**: permutation importance (decrease in ROC-AUC over three repeats on a stratified 20,000-record test subsample) was compared with the SHAP ranking. Finally, for error analysis, test records were grouped into true positives (TP), true negatives (TN), false positives (FP) and false negatives (FN) at the validation-F1 threshold, and the mean SHAP profile of up to 400 records per group was computed.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 4
h1("4. Results")
h2("4.1. Detection Performance and Comparison with Baselines")
p("Table 3 reports test performance on UNSW-NB15 at the conventional threshold of 0.5 and at the validation false-alarm budget. At τ = 0.5, all strong models fall within a narrow band: F1 between 89.0% and 89.7% and ROC-AUC between 0.978 and 0.984. FTT-IDS (F1 89.45 ± 0.60%, ROC-AUC 0.9841 ± 0.0014) matches XGBoost (89.50 ± 0.04%, 0.9839) and RF (89.43 ± 0.03%, 0.9835). Its ranking quality is equal to or higher than that of the neural baselines, including FT-Transformer (ROC-AUC 0.9820). All models share the same failure mode at this threshold: recall is close to 99%, but roughly a quarter of the normal test traffic (24.7–28.3%) is flagged as malicious. The MSc thesis reported the same pattern for its single-seed model (accuracy 86.99%, FPR 27.6%).")
