h2("4.5. Explanation Reliability")
p("**Global explanations.** Figure 5 shows the global SHAP importance of FTT-IDS on both datasets. On UNSW-NB15, the explanations are dominated by three TTL-derived features (*ct_state_ttl*, *sttl* and *dttl*), followed by *service* and *proto*. This agrees with the single-seed ranking of the MSc thesis (*sttl*, *ct_state_ttl*, *state*) but is more concentrated on TTL information. On TON_IoT, packet and byte counts (*dst_pkts*, *src_pkts*, *dst_bytes*), the connection state and the duration dominate. The relative additivity error of the expected-gradients estimates was 13–17% on UNSW-NB15 and 13–19% on TON_IoT. Individual attributions are therefore approximate, which is a further argument for the aggregate, multi-seed view used here.")
fig("fig6_global_shap.png", "Global SHAP importance of FTT-IDS on UNSW-NB15 (left) and TON_IoT (right): mean absolute SHAP value of the attack margin over 2000 test records, averaged over five seeds (error bars: s.d. across seeds).")
p("**Stability.** Table 9 summarises the stability results. For a fixed model, explanations were almost insensitive to the choice of background sample (Spearman ρ ≥ 0.99). Across independently trained models, stability was high on TON_IoT (ρ = 0.95) but only moderate on UNSW-NB15 (ρ = 0.76, Kendall τ = 0.60), and the top-10 feature sets of two seeds had a Jaccard overlap of only 0.57, i.e., about seven common features. The MSc model (A1) behaved similarly (ρ = 0.75). The large error bars in Figure 5 show the same effect: two models with practically identical accuracy can distribute importance differently among correlated TTL features. A single-seed SHAP ranking, as reported in most XAI-NIDS studies, is therefore one draw from a distribution of plausible explanations.")
table("Table 9. Reliability of FTT-IDS explanations (mean ± s.d.). Faithfulness: test ROC-AUC after masking the k most important vs. k random features (no retraining).",
      ["Property", "Statistic", "UNSW-NB15", "TON_IoT"],
      [["Stability across backgrounds", "Spearman ρ / top-10 Jaccard", "0.998 / 0.91", "0.990 / 0.82"],
       ["Stability across 5 seeds (10 pairs)", "Spearman ρ", "0.764 ± 0.062", "0.949 ± 0.015"],
       ["", "Kendall τ", "0.597 ± 0.058", "0.828 ± 0.026"],
       ["", "Top-10 Jaccard", "0.568 ± 0.095", "0.727 ± 0.078"],
       ["Agreement with permutation importance", "Spearman ρ / top-10 overlap", "0.760 / 7 of 10", "0.861 / 8 of 10"],
       ["Faithfulness, k = 5", "AUC: SHAP top-k vs. random k", "0.738 vs. 0.980", "0.824 vs. 0.971"],
       ["Faithfulness, k = 10", "AUC: SHAP top-k vs. random k", "0.746 vs. 0.922", "0.648 vs. 0.934"],
       ["Local deletion, k = 5 (seed 42)", "Alert flip rate: SHAP vs. random", "7.5% vs. 2.6%", "0.3% vs. 0.6%"]],
      [2900, 2700, 1850, 1850])
p("**Faithfulness.** Figure 6 shows that masking the globally most important features degrades the model far more than masking random features. On UNSW-NB15, masking the top five features lowers ROC-AUC from 0.984 to 0.738, while masking five random features leaves it at 0.980; on TON_IoT the corresponding values are 0.824 and 0.971. The global rankings therefore identify features on which the model genuinely depends. Local, per-alert deletion was informative on UNSW-NB15 (masking an alert's own top five features flipped 7.5% of alerts versus 2.6% for random features, and lowered the attack probability by 0.137 versus 0.015). It was uninformative on TON_IoT, where alerts are supported by many redundant features and no single small set of features changes the decision. Permutation importance, a model-agnostic method, agreed with SHAP on seven (UNSW-NB15) and eight (TON_IoT) of the top-10 features.")
fig("fig7_faithfulness.png", "Faithfulness of global SHAP rankings: test ROC-AUC of FTT-IDS after replacing the k most important features (blue) or k random features (orange) by reference values, without retraining (mean ± s.d. over five seeds; random masks also averaged over five draws).")

h2("4.6. What Explains the False Alarms?")
p("Figure 7 shows the mean SHAP profile of each prediction outcome on UNSW-NB15. False positives look like true positives in the TTL features: *ct_state_ttl* and *sttl* push false alarms towards the attack class (+2.46 and +2.16) almost as strongly as genuine attacks (+3.25 and +3.01), whereas these features push true negatives firmly towards normal (−2.42 and −2.26). For 56% of the explained false alarms, *sttl* is the single largest contributor. The non-TTL features (for example *service* and *proto*) instead point weakly towards normal in false alarms. In other words, the model raises these alerts *because of* their TTL values.")
fig("fig8_shap_by_outcome.png", "Mean SHAP value of the ten globally most important features for true positives (TP), false negatives (FN), false positives (FP) and true negatives (TN) of FTT-IDS on UNSW-NB15 (seed 42, validation-F1 threshold; up to 400 records per group). Red pushes towards an alert, blue towards normal.")
p("Figure 8 shows the data behind this behaviour. In the de-duplicated training data, 75% of normal flows have *sttl* = 31 and only 19% have *sttl* = 254, whereas 75% of attacks have *sttl* = 254. In the official test set, the share of normal flows with *sttl* = 254 doubles to 41%. Of the false alarms of FTT-IDS at τ = 0.5, 88% are normal flows with *sttl* = 254, most of them with state FIN (72%) and no identified service (84%).")
fig("fig5_sttl_shift.png", "Distribution of the source TTL (sttl) by partition and class in UNSW-NB15 (training data after de-duplication; official test set). Normal test traffic contains a much larger share of sttl = 254 flows than normal training traffic.")
p("A natural remedy would be to remove the TTL features. To test this, FTT-IDS and XGBoost were retrained with five seeds without *sttl* and *ct_state_ttl*, and again without all three TTL features (*sttl*, *dttl* and *ct_state_ttl*). Neither intervention reduced false alarms. Without any TTL feature, FTT-IDS reached F1 89.77% and FPR 24.98% at τ = 0.5 (93.17% and 4.85% at the budget threshold); XGBoost reached 89.29% and 26.45% (93.21% and 5.86%). Moreover, the false alarms of the TTL-free models are largely the *same flows*. The Jaccard overlap between the false-alarm sets of the full and the TTL-free FTT-IDS was 0.77 on average over seeds. In addition, 88% of the TTL-free model's false alarms still have *sttl* = 254, even though the model never saw this feature. The TTL fields are therefore not the cause of the errors but the most legible proxy for a sub-population of test traffic that is under-represented among normal training flows. Without them, the model recognises the same population through correlated features. SHAP correctly points to the proxy; feature removal does not fix the underlying shift. The validation-budget threshold, which acts on the decision rather than the representation, remains the effective control.")

h2("4.7. Model Size and Inference Cost")
p("FTT-IDS has 577,410 trainable parameters on UNSW-NB15; most of them belong to the 2048-wide feed-forward blocks inherited from the MSc configuration. FT-Transformer has 937,538 parameters, the MLP 117,858 and the 1D-CNN 178,946. On a laptop CPU, scoring a single flow took a median of 1.43 ms with FTT-IDS, compared with 2.49 ms with FT-Transformer and 0.31 ms with the MLP and the 1D-CNN. On the GPU, FTT-IDS scored about 95,000 flows per second in batches of 1024. No claim of a lightweight design is made. These figures only indicate that per-flow scoring is not a bottleneck for offline or near-real-time analysis on commodity hardware.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 5
h1("5. Discussion")
p("**Accuracy is not the differentiator.** Under a protocol that removes duplicate-induced leakage and gives every model a comparable tuning budget, the feature-token Transformer matches but does not beat gradient-boosted trees on UNSW-NB15, and it trails them slightly on TON_IoT. This result is consistent with tabular-learning benchmarks {grinsztajn,shwartz}. It also suggests that some of the near-perfect accuracies reported for Transformer-based NIDSs may owe more to evaluation choices than to architecture {arp}. The ablations still clarify when attention helps. Tokenising features and giving each token an identity is clearly better than collapsing the record into a single vector, and the form of the embedding is of secondary importance. For practitioners, the choice between a Transformer and a tree ensemble can therefore rest on other criteria, such as the availability of gradient-based explanations, integration into end-to-end pipelines or the need for very low latency.")
p("**Operating points matter more than models.** The largest effect in this study comes from the decision rule. Setting the threshold at a validation false-alarm budget removes over 80% of false alarms for every model and raises F1 by about four points. Changing the model alters F1 by less than one point. Thresholds that optimise F1 or Youden's index on validation data do not achieve this. These rules balance errors under the validation class distribution, whereas the cost that matters operationally is the alert volume on benign traffic {axelsson,apruzzese}. Reporting results only at τ = 0.5 or as ROC-AUC therefore hides a practically important degree of freedom. NIDS studies should state the decision rule explicitly and report FPR at a fixed operating point.")
p("**Explanations need their own evaluation.** The SHAP rankings of FTT-IDS passed the faithfulness and model-agnostic agreement checks, which supports their use for feature-level analysis. They were nevertheless only moderately stable across retrained models on UNSW-NB15, a finding that matches the retraining-stability results reported for tree models by Maseno et al. {maseno}. Analysts who receive a single-model explanation should not over-interpret the precise ranking of correlated features; aggregating explanations over several models, as done here, gives a more reliable picture. The most useful application of SHAP in this study was diagnostic. Contrasting the explanations of true and false positives revealed that false alarms are driven by TTL values, which in turn exposed a train–test shift in the benchmark. This is the type of shortcut that the XAI literature warns about {geirhos,lapuschkin}. The study also illustrates a limit of explanations: identifying the proxy feature did not make the problem removable at the feature level.")
p("**Limitations.** First, only two datasets were used, both produced by the same research group and both with flow features; conclusions may differ for packet-level models and for datasets such as CICIoT2023 or Edge-IIoTset {neto,ferrag}. Second, the study addresses binary detection; the per-category analysis covers multi-class behaviour only indirectly. Third, the hyperparameter grids were deliberately small and equal in size; larger searches could change the ranking of close competitors, although they are unlikely to change the effect of the decision rule. Fourth, the expected-gradients estimator had a relative additivity error of 13–19%, so individual attributions are approximate. Finally, the false-alarm budget is set on validation data drawn from the training distribution. Under the train–test shift observed in UNSW-NB15, the realised test FPR (4.4%) exceeded the 1% budget, which shows that periodic recalibration on recent benign traffic would be needed in deployment.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 6
h1("6. Conclusions")
p("This study re-evaluated a SHAP-explained feature-token Transformer for network intrusion detection under a protocol designed to avoid common evaluation pitfalls: duplicate removal before splitting, validation-only model and threshold selection, five seeds with paired significance tests, a test-set sensitivity analysis and an audit of explanation reliability. On UNSW-NB15 and TON_IoT, the Transformer was competitive with, but not superior to, tuned gradient-boosted trees. Its key component was the feature-identity information of each token. A decision threshold fixed at a 1% validation false-alarm budget lowered the UNSW-NB15 test false-positive rate from 26.6% to 4.4% and raised F1 from 89.5% to 93.7%; the effect held for every model and on de-duplicated test data. SHAP explanations were faithful but only moderately stable across retrained models. They showed that false alarms are driven by TTL features acting as proxies for a train–test shift, and removing those features did not remove the shift. Future work will extend the protocol to multi-class detection and further IoT datasets, study recalibration of the false-alarm budget under drift, and investigate training objectives that reduce reliance on proxy features.")

